C42 (36-3, Japan) Determine all integers $n>3$, such that there exist $n$ points $A_{1}, A_{2}, \cdots, A_{n}$ in the plane and real numbers $r_{1}, r_{2}, \cdots, r_{n}$, satisfying the following two conditions:
(1) No three points of $A_{1}, A_{2}, \cdots, A_{n}$ are collinear;
(2) For each triple $i, j, k(1 \leqslant i<j<k \leqslant n), \triangle A_{i} A_{j} A_{k}$ has area equal to $r_{i}+r_{j}+r_{k}$.

